@page "/ai-transparency"
@layout MarketingLayout

<HeadContent>
    <SeoHeadContent Path="/ai-transparency"
                    Title="AI & Your Data — Brainy"
                    Description="Exactly what leaves Brainy when an AI feature runs: which provider, what content, and why AI output must be reviewed." />
</HeadContent>

<section class="mkt-hero">
    <div class="mkt-wrap">
        <span class="mkt-eyebrow mkt-rise">Trust</span>
        <h1 class="mkt-title mkt-rise mkt-d1" style="max-width: 16em; font-size: clamp(2.2rem, 4.5vw, 3.4rem);">
            AI &amp; your data
        </h1>
        <p class="mkt-lead mkt-rise mkt-d2" style="margin-top: 1.1rem;">
            What leaves Brainy when you trigger an AI feature, in concrete terms &mdash; not marketing language.
        </p>
        <div class="mkt-draft-notice mkt-rise mkt-d3" role="note">
            <span class="mkt-draft-notice__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#9888;&#65039;</span>
            <p>
                <strong>Draft &mdash; pending qualified legal review.</strong> This page describes Brainy's actual
                AI implementation as of today. It is product documentation, not a finalized legal disclosure, and
                is subject to review before paid acquisition begins.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section class="mkt-section">
    <div class="mkt-wrap">
        <article class="mkt-changelog">

            <h2>AI is off by default</h2>
            <p>
                A Brainy deployment ships with its AI provider set to <strong>None</strong>. In that mode, every
                AI feature returns a plain "no AI provider is configured" message and <strong>nothing you write is
                ever sent anywhere</strong>. AI only activates when an operator explicitly configures a provider
                (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) and only runs when you personally click an AI action &mdash; it never
                runs in the background on your content.
            </p>

            <h2>Which providers can be configured</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>OpenAI</strong> &mdash; the public OpenAI API (api.openai.com).</li>
                <li><strong>Azure OpenAI Service</strong> &mdash; a private Azure OpenAI resource and deployment.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                There is no other AI subprocessor. If a deployment is configured with a provider, every AI call
                goes to that one provider using one model, set by the deployment's configuration.
            </p>

            <h2>What is sent, action by action</h2>
            <p>Brainy currently offers five AI actions. Each one sends only the text relevant to that action &mdash; never your whole workspace:</p>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Summarize a note</strong> &mdash; sends that note's content.</li>
                <li><strong>Suggest PARA placement &amp; tags</strong> &mdash; sends that note's title and content.</li>
                <li><strong>Extract action items</strong> &mdash; sends that note's content.</li>
                <li><strong>Detect duplicate notes</strong> &mdash; sends the note you're checking (title and content) plus the titles and existing summaries of your other candidate notes.</li>
                <li><strong>Generate an output draft</strong> (post, report, brief) &mdash; sends the output's title, type, and the full content of every source note you selected for that draft.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Each action is initiated by you, on demand, for a specific note, task, or output you are looking
                at &mdash; you always choose the scope by choosing which item(s) to run it on.
            </p>

            <h2>What comes back, and how it's labeled</h2>
            <p>
                Every AI result is returned with the model that produced it and a prompt version identifier, and
                Brainy keeps these attached to the generated content separately from your own edits. AI-generated
                suggestions, summaries, and drafts are shown labeled as AI-generated in the interface, alongside
                the sources that produced them, so you can see what came from you and what came from the model.
            </p>

            <h2>AI can be wrong &mdash; review before you rely on it</h2>
            <p>
                Language models can misread context, omit important details, or state something confidently that
                is not true. Brainy does not treat AI output as fact: it is a draft. Review AI-generated summaries,
                suggestions, and outputs against their sources before you act on them, share them, or let them
                replace your own judgment.
            </p>

            <h2>Training</h2>
            <p>
                Brainy does not use your content to train its own models, and does not fine-tune any model on
                your data. Whether a configured third-party provider (OpenAI or Azure OpenAI) itself retains or
                trains on API request content is governed by that provider's own API terms, which apply
                independently of this page &mdash; Azure OpenAI's data handling terms differ from the public
                OpenAI API's, which is one reason the choice of provider is an explicit deployment setting rather
                than fixed.
            </p>

            <h2>Limiting or avoiding AI</h2>
            <p>
                Every AI feature is optional and explicit &mdash; there is no AI action that runs without you
                choosing to trigger it. If you would rather nothing you write is ever sent to an AI provider,
                simply do not use the AI actions described above; the rest of Brainy (capture, PARA organization,
                tasks, Today, search) works fully without them.
            </p>

            <p>
                See also the <a href="/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> for how the rest of your data is stored, and
                <a href="/Account/Manage">Account and data</a> to export or delete your data at any time.
            </p>

        </article>
    </div>
</section>
